DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmeemmsseett() function writes _l_e_n bytes of value _c (converted to an
     _u_n_s_i_g_n_e_d _c_h_a_r) to the string _d_e_s_t.  Undefined behaviour from mmeemmsseett(),
     resulting from storage overflow, will occur if _l_e_n is greater than the
     length of the _d_e_s_t buffer.  The behaviour is also undefined if _d_e_s_t is an
     invalid pointer.

     The mmeemmsseett__ss() function behaves the same as mmeemmsseett() except that an error
     is returned and the currently registered runtime-constraint handler is
     called if _d_e_s_t is a null pointer, _d_e_s_t_s_z or _l_e_n is greater than
     RSIZE_MAX, or _l_e_n is greater than _d_e_s_t_s_z (buffer overflow would occur).
     The runtime-constraint handler is called first and may not return.  If it
     does return, an error is returned to the caller.  Like explicit_bzero(3),
     mmeemmsseett__ss() is not removed through Dead Store Elimination (DSE), making it
     useful for clearing sensitive data.  In contrast mmeemmsseett() function may be
     optimized away if the object modified by the function is not accessed
     again.  To clear memory that will not subsequently be accessed it is ad‐
     vised to use mmeemmsseett__ss() instead of mmeemmsseett().  For instance, a buffer con‐
     taining a password should be cleared with mmeemmsseett__ss() before free(3).

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The mmeemmsseett() function returns its first argument.  The mmeemmsseett__ss() func‐
     tion returns zero on success, non-zero on error.

